# Hex bolt with an ISO metric thread (M16 x P2)
#
# Head at the bottom (prints flat, no supports), the thread cut straight into
# the shank. M16 coarse (JIS B 0205 / ISO 261: d16 x P2) with the head from
# JIS B 1180 (across flats s = 24, height k = 10). The coarse 2 mm pitch is
# what makes the thread printable: a 1.5 mm deep groove with a 0.25 mm flat on
# the crest clears a 0.4 mm nozzle, where M8's P1.25 does not.
#
# Two OCCT lessons are baked into the groove profile:
# - The profile overhangs the shank surface (lead) instead of exactly touching
#   it. A tool tangent to the surface leaves micron-thin sliver solids in the
#   grooves; overhanging the same flank lines cuts the identical groove with
#   zero debris.
# - Adjacent turns of the tool must not touch each other. The crest is the
#   land left between neighbouring grooves; with land = 0 the swept tool is
#   tangent to itself turn-to-turn and a later boolean silently returns
#   nothing. ISO truncates the crest to pitch/8 for good reason. The guard is
#   hw * (lead + gd) / gd < pitch / 2, which is why `lead` shrinks with `land`.
#

pitch = 2
r = 8          # shank radius: d16 / 2
shank = 30     # shank length


head_h = 10
hr = 13.856    # hex head circumradius: across flats 24 / sqrt(3)

# 2026-10-02: the `thread` primitive builds the threaded shank directly
# (one ruled face per flank per turn, no swept groove, no boolean against the
# cylinder). Same ISO profile as the swept groove it replaces: crest land pitch/8, 60-degree flanks to a
# sharp root (depth (pitch - land) / 2 * sqrt(3), the default). The thread runs to the head (全ねじ).
# chamfer:true cuts the tip at 45 degrees down to the thread root (ISO 4753
# type CH), which is what lets a nut start.
shaft = thread r pitch shank chamfer:true
 | floor
 | translate 0 0 head_h

head = polygon 6 hr
 | extrude head_h
 | faces <Z | chamfer 1

union [head, shaft]
